  "css": "/* The set's one color, for the stamp's field. The title sets the same red\n   in its own area, and card 2's band in its cell — which the title then\n   takes its color from, so changing the cell changes both. */\n:root {\n  --accent-color: #d0362b;\n}\n\n/* The stamp's field: the area's fill layer, inset to the padding. */\n#sketch .surface {\n  background: var(--accent-color);\n}\n\n/* The page number takes the page's own face; this sets it in the mono of the footer. */\n.page-number {\n  font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace !important;\n  font-size: 6.5pt !important;\n  letter-spacing: 0.3mm;\n  color: #8a857b !important;\n}\n\n/* Headings inside the body in the title's serif. */\n#body h1, #body h2, #body h3 {\n  font-family: 'Fraunces', serif;\n  letter-spacing: -0.1mm;\n}\n\n/* `code` in the footer's mono, on a faint chip. */\n#body code {\n  font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace;\n  font-size: 0.86em;\n  background: #efece4;\n  padding: 0 0.6mm;\n  border-radius: 0.6mm;\n}\n\n/* The drawing's stamp, lifted off the page. A drop-shadow follows what is\n   painted, so it traces the perforations; a box-shadow would be a rectangle.\n   The first four are a 1px hairline all round — white paper on white paper\n   needs an edge — and the last is the shadow, cast by paper and hairline\n   together, since each filter works on what the one before it drew. */\n#sketch {\n  filter:\n    drop-shadow(1px 0 0 #eee) drop-shadow(-1px 0 0 #eee)\n    drop-shadow(0 1px 0 #eee) drop-shadow(0 -1px 0 #eee)\n    drop-shadow(0.5mm 0.7mm 0.2mm rgba(40, 30, 10, 0.3));\n}\n\n/* The post-it off the page's edge: a curl of shadow under its lower corners,\n   and a strip of darker yellow where the glue is. Never printed — it is off\n   the paper — so it is only ever the editor's. */\n#notes {\n  box-shadow: 0 1.6mm 2.2mm -1mm rgba(60, 45, 0, 0.35);\n}\n#notes::after {\n  content: '';\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0 0 auto 0;\n  height: 6mm;\n  background: rgba(190, 160, 0, 0.08);\n}\n\n/* The post-it as it is in daylight, whatever the theme: a yellow note,\n   dark words. Dark turns the page back by itself; the inverted page turns\n   the words over but not the fill, so there the note is turned back as a\n   whole — and its fill, which the app had already turned back, is let be\n   rather than turned a second time. Screen only: what prints is light. */\n.theme-dark-page #notes {\n  filter: invert(1) hue-rotate(180deg);\n}\n.theme-dark-page #notes .surface {\n  filter: none !important;\n}\n\n#body mark {\n  background: #f6e27a;\n  padding: 0 0.4mm;\n}\n",
  "locked": true
}
